From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 17:53:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: don't submit to a GPU that failed to resume

msm_gpu_submit() calls pm_runtime_get_sync() and msm_gpu_hw_init() and
throws both return values away, then goes on to touch the hardware
regardless.  If the GPU did not come back, everything the submit path
does to it from that point on -- a5xx_hw_init()'s register programming,
then the CP_RB_WPTR write out of a5xx_flush() -- is a bus transaction
aimed at a block whose head switches are down.

On msm8998 the interconnect answers those with an external abort, and
because the writes are posted it arrives as an asynchronous SError some
microseconds later, at whatever the CPU happens to be executing by then.
That is a machine check, not an error the driver is given a chance to
handle:

  SError Interrupt on CPU0, code 0x00000000bf000002 -- SError
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Asynchronous SError Interrupt
  Tainted: G   M            [M]=MACHINE_CHECK
  Workqueue: ring0 drm_sched_run_job_work [gpu_sched]
    el1h_64_irq+0x6c/0x70
    adreno_wait_ring+0x8/0xac [msm]
    msm_gpu_submit+0x6c/0x288 [msm]
    msm_job_run+0xb0/0x134 [msm]
    drm_sched_run_job_work+0x2ec/0x4b8 [gpu_sched]

adreno_wait_ring() is where the abort was delivered, not where it was
raised -- it is reported at an IRQ entry taken inside the frame, and on
this part the ring-space poll reads the CP_WHERE_AM_I shadow out of
memory rather than a register.  What it does tell us is that the CPU was
already several register writes into the submit before the fault landed.

That the GPU had no power at that point is not a guess.  The IOMMU core
links the GPU to its SMMU with a runtime-PM device link, and on this SoC
the SMMU is what owns the GPU_CX and GPU_GX GDSCs:

  gpu_gx  off-0
      5040000.iommu     suspended
  gpu_cx  off-0
  5000000.gpu  supplier:platform:5040000.iommu  runtime_pm=1

A get that succeeds therefore guarantees the head switches are up before
the driver callback runs.  A bus transaction the interconnect refuses
means the get did not succeed and the driver carried on anyway.

Downstream never allows this.  Every kgsl register accessor calls
kgsl_pre_hwaccess() first, which asserts kgsl_state_is_awake() with the
comment "a register access without device power will cause a fatal
timeout".  Adopt the same contract: if the GPU is not up, do not touch
it.

Fail the submit instead, and signal its hardware fence with the error --
the job never reached the ring, so nothing would otherwise retire it and
every waiter on that frame would hang.  Clear the latched runtime error
on the way out as well, or the first failed wake would leave
dev->power.runtime_error set and every later get would return it without
retrying, keeping the GPU down for the rest of the boot.

This does not stop a resume from failing; it stops a failed resume from
taking the machine down with it, and makes it say which error it was.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c | 42 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c
index dd8caad960ad..22f0f4a6552d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c
@@ -886,12 +886,41 @@ void msm_gpu_submit(struct msm_gpu *gpu, struct msm_gem_submit *submit)
 {
 	struct msm_ringbuffer *ring = submit->ring;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
 
 	WARN_ON(!mutex_is_locked(&gpu->lock));
 
-	pm_runtime_get_sync(&gpu->pdev->dev);
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(&gpu->pdev->dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * Nothing below this point may run if the GPU did not come
+		 * back.  The submit's very first hardware access is the
+		 * CP_RB_RPTR read that adreno_wait_ring() polls before the
+		 * first packet is written, and on a GPU whose head switches
+		 * are down that read is answered by the interconnect with an
+		 * external abort.  It arrives as an asynchronous SError, i.e.
+		 * a machine check the driver never gets to handle.
+		 */
+		dev_err_ratelimited(&gpu->pdev->dev,
+				    "failed to resume the GPU (%d), dropping submit\n",
+				    ret);
+		/*
+		 * A failed resume latches into dev->power.runtime_error and
+		 * every later get returns it without retrying, so one bad
+		 * wake would keep the GPU down for the rest of the boot.
+		 * Clear it and let the next frame try again.
+		 */
+		pm_runtime_set_suspended(&gpu->pdev->dev);
+		goto fail;
+	}
 
-	msm_gpu_hw_init(gpu);
+	ret = msm_gpu_hw_init(gpu);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err_ratelimited(&gpu->pdev->dev,
+				    "hw init failed (%d), dropping submit\n", ret);
+		pm_runtime_put(&gpu->pdev->dev);
+		goto fail;
+	}
 
 	submit->seqno = submit->hw_fence->seqno;
 
@@ -921,6 +950,15 @@ void msm_gpu_submit(struct msm_gpu *gpu, struct msm_gem_submit *submit)
 
 	pm_runtime_put(&gpu->pdev->dev);
 	hangcheck_timer_reset(gpu);
+	return;
+
+fail:
+	/*
+	 * The job never reached the ring, so nothing will ever retire it.
+	 * Signal it here or every waiter on this frame hangs forever.
+	 */
+	dma_fence_set_error(submit->hw_fence, ret);
+	dma_fence_signal(submit->hw_fence);
 }
 
 /*
